C = [
("h1", "11. “Shall First Come” and “Is Come Already”"),
("p", "Claim: the Lord’s two sentences in Matthew 17:11 and 17:12 are not a contradiction and are not one statement said twice. The first is about a coming still ahead when he spoke it, by a man who restores all things. The second is about a coming already past, by a man whom the people did not know and who suffered. The chapter holds that the text leads to a further coming of Elijah before the day of the LORD, in the line of Israel, and sets out the reasons in order: the order of events, the words of the Lord, the form of Gabriel’s sentence, the man on the mountain, and the passage in Revelation 11 that the volume cites and does not name."),
("sub", "11.1 What Malachi 4:5 fixes"),
("p", "Malachi’s promise has one time mark, and it is “before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD” (Malachi 4:5). Elijah is sent before the day. The promise does not say how long before, and it does not say that the sending is fulfilled in one lifetime or in one visit. The test the volume applies is the test of Malachi’s own sentence: what is it that must come first, and has the day come? The Gospels record that John came and preached and was put to death. They do not record that the great and dreadful day followed. A promise that Elijah comes before the day is met by a coming that precedes the day, and the day, in the Gospels and after them, has not come."),
("sub", "11.2 The order of events"),
("p", "Matthew tells of John’s death in chapter 14, three chapters before the Lord says “Elias truly shall first come” (Matthew 14:10; 17:11). The sentence is spoken after John has been beheaded. A speaker who says “shall first come” after the man in question has died is, on its plain reading, speaking of a coming still to be. If the Lord meant that John’s ministry had already accomplished the coming, the future tense would have no work to do, and the disciples, who had watched John’s ministry end, would have had no reason to ask. The question the disciples ask is also in the present, “say the scribes that Elias must first come,” and the Lord does not say that the scribes’ teaching has been met. He says that it is so."),
("p", "The second sentence does not take back the first. It adds a fact: “Elias is come already, and they knew him not.” The adversative “But I say unto you” sets the second sentence against what the disciples might conclude from the first, that nothing has yet been done. The Lord corrects that conclusion. Something has been done, and it was not recognized. The volume holds both sentences in the order in which the Lord gave them, the first in the future and the second in the past."),
("sub", "11.3 The words “restore all things”"),
("p", "The work the Lord names is “restore all things.” Malachi 4:6 speaks of turning hearts, and the Lord’s phrase is wider. It speaks of everything. The Gospels do not report that John’s ministry restored all things. They report the opposite: a ministry of repentance that drew the crowds, that the leaders refused, and that ended in a prison and a beheading (Matthew 14:3–10). The Lord himself says of the generation that it refused both John and the Son of man (Matthew 11:16–19). The nation was not turned and the land was not restored. The Lord’s phrase has a counterpart in Peter’s word in the temple that the heaven must receive Jesus “until the times of restitution of all things, which God hath spoken by the mouth of all his holy prophets since the world began” (Acts 3:21). That restitution is spoken of as still ahead after the cross and the resurrection, and it is Israel’s: Peter speaks to “ye men of Israel” (Acts 3:12) and to those who “are the children of the prophets, and of the covenant” (Acts 3:25). The volume reads the Lord’s “restore all things” as the work of the coming that is still ahead."),
("sub", "11.4 The form of Gabriel’s sentence"),
("p", "Gabriel’s sentence has been read in chapter 6. Its form is the form the Old Testament used of Elisha, a man who bears another’s spirit and is not that man. The sentence is “in the spirit and power of Elias,” and not “as Elias.” If the angel had meant that John would be Elijah, the Old Testament’s own precedent for the form is against him; if he meant that John would do Elijah’s work and bear Elijah’s spirit, the form fits. The sentence thus implies a distinct man: the Elias whose spirit and power John bore. The volume reads Luke 1:17 as consistent with Matthew 17:11 for this reason. A man who goes before the Lord in Elijah’s spirit stands in an order of such men, and the Elias himself is not that man."),
("p", "The same point stands behind John’s “I am not.” John says that he is not Elias, and the Lord says that Elias is come already. Chapter 7 held that the volume does not reconcile the two statements beyond the text. What the volume adds here is that the statements fit a coming of Elias in the person of one who bore his spirit, and that this coming is not the whole of what the Lord calls “shall first come.” John is Elias in the sense of Matthew 11:14, if the nation will receive it. John is not Elias in the sense of John 1:21, the person the priests asked about. Neither sentence needs to be rewritten for the reading, and neither is made to say more than it does."),
("sub", "11.5 The man on the mountain"),
("p", "On the mount of transfiguration, “there appeared unto them Moses and Elias talking with him” (Matthew 17:3). The text names a person, Elias, who appears, and in the same scene, a few verses later, the Lord speaks of Elias as one who “shall first come” and as one who “is come already.” The disciples saw Elias. They did not take him for John, for John was dead and they were told afterwards that the Lord spoke of John. The volume does not build a theory on the appearance. It notes that in the same scene the Gospels name Elias as a person who appears, as one who comes first, and as one who has come, and that the three are not the same use of the name."),
("sub", "11.6 Revelation 11"),
("p", "The last text the volume sets beside the question is Revelation 11. “And I will give power unto my two witnesses, and they shall prophesy a thousand two hundred and threescore days, clothed in sackcloth” (Revelation 11:3). Then: “if any man will hurt them, fire proceedeth out of their mouth, and devoureth their enemies” (Revelation 11:5), and “These have power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy” (Revelation 11:6). The next verses tell that the beast shall overcome them and kill them, that after three days and a half “the Spirit of life from God entered into them, and they stood upon their feet,” and that they heard “a great voice from heaven saying unto them, Come up hither. And they ascended up to heaven in a cloud” (Revelation 11:7–12). The passage opens with the measuring of the temple and the holy city given to the Gentiles “forty and two months” (Revelation 11:1, 2)."),
("p", "The volume sets out the features and does not name the witnesses. The fire from the mouth is the fire of 2 Kings 1. The power to shut heaven is the power of 1 Kings 17:1. The days, one thousand two hundred and threescore, are three years and a half by the count of thirty days to the month, the same span James gives to the shutting of the heaven in Elijah’s day (James 5:17) and the Lord gives in Luke 4:25. The ascent into heaven in a cloud after a death is the end of a prophet’s ministry, as the whirlwind was Elijah’s. The setting is a temple at Jerusalem and a city given to the Gentiles, the setting of Malachi’s own paragraphs, and the volume reads it as Israel’s line. The passage does not call anyone Elijah, and the volume does not add the name. The Lord’s two sentences say that Elias shall first come, and Revelation 11 shows prophets of Elijah’s works in the days of the beast. The volume lays them side by side."),
("sub", "11.7 What the volume holds, and what it does not say"),
("p", "The position is this. Malachi 4:5 promises Elijah the prophet before the day of the LORD. John went before the Lord in his spirit and power and was, if the nation would receive it, Elias. The nation did not receive it, John was put to death, and the Lord spoke afterwards of Elias as one who shall first come and restore all things. The restoring is not reported done and the day has not come. The text therefore points to a further coming of Elijah before the day, in Israel’s line, as the restorer. The Body of Christ is not read into any of these passages."),
("p", "The volume does not say when this coming is. It does not say where. It does not say that the person who comes is the Tishbite of Kings in the flesh, and it does not say that he is another man in Elijah’s spirit. It does not identify the witnesses of Revelation 11. It does not set a date for the day of the LORD, which is read in Study 7 and in the fifth volume of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series. It does not say that every sentence about Elijah in the Gospels is about the further coming. The position is as large as the text allows and no larger."),
("ctbl", "The reasons for a further coming, in order.", [
    ("Reason", "Text", "What it shows"),
    ("The time mark", "Malachi 4:5", "Elijah before the day; the day has not come"),
    ("The order of events", "Matthew 14:10; 17:11", "“Shall first come” is spoken after John’s death"),
    ("The work", "Matthew 17:11; Acts 3:21", "“Restore all things” is not reported done; restitution lies ahead"),
    ("The form of Gabriel’s sentence", "Luke 1:17; 2 Kings 2:15", "A man in Elijah’s spirit, not Elijah"),
    ("John’s answer and the Lord’s", "John 1:21; Matthew 11:14", "Two statements, each in its own setting, not made to agree beyond the text"),
    ("The man on the mount", "Matthew 17:3", "Elias is a named person who appears"),
    ("Elijah’s works later", "Revelation 11:3–12", "Fire from the mouth, a shut heaven, the days, the ascent; set beside, not named"),
  ], [0.26, 0.28, 0.46]),
("sub", "11.8 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Malachi 4:5 puts Elijah “before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD,” and the day has not come.",
    "“Elias truly shall first come” is spoken after John’s death, and “restore all things” is not reported done by John’s ministry; Acts 3:21 speaks of restitution as still ahead.",
    "“Elias is come already” is a second fact set against the first, and John’s “I am not” and the Lord’s “this is Elias” are each read in their own setting.",
    "Luke 1:17 uses the form the Old Testament used of Elisha, which implies a distinct man whose spirit and power John bore.",
    "The text points to a further coming of Elijah before the day, in Israel’s line; Revelation 11 is cited beside it and its witnesses are not named.",
  ]),
]
